# KAF81 Website - Cursor Rules

## Project Overview
This is a static website for KAF81 (KASI 1981 Alumni Foundation) built with vanilla HTML, CSS, and JavaScript. No frameworks or libraries are used except for Google Fonts.

## Technology Stack
- HTML5 (Semantic)
- CSS3 (Vanilla, no preprocessors)
- JavaScript (Vanilla ES6+, no frameworks)
- Supabase (for contact form and scholarship application form backend)

## Code Style and Structure

### HTML
- Use semantic HTML5 elements (`<header>`, `<nav>`, `<main>`, `<section>`, `<article>`, `<aside>`, `<footer>`)
- Proper heading hierarchy (h1 → h2 → h3, no skipping levels)
- Always include `lang="en"` on `<html>` tag
- Include viewport meta tag: `<meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">`
- All images must have descriptive `alt` attributes
- Use descriptive link text (avoid "click here")
- Include skip to main content link for accessibility

### CSS
- Mobile-first responsive design approach
- Use CSS custom properties (CSS variables) for theming
- Typography:
  - Headings: `font-family: 'Playfair Display', serif;`
  - Body: `font-family: 'Quicksand', sans-serif;`
- Use CSS Grid and Flexbox for layouts
- Use meaningful class names (BEM-like but not strict BEM)
- Include focus states for all interactive elements
- Support `prefers-reduced-motion` media query
- Ensure color contrast ratio ≥ 4.5:1 for accessibility

### JavaScript
- Use vanilla JavaScript only (no libraries or frameworks)
- Use `'use strict';` in all JS files
- Use the `function` keyword for pure functions
- Use descriptive variable names with auxiliary verbs (e.g., `isLoading`, `hasError`)
- Prefer const over let, avoid var
- Use curly braces for all conditionals
- Write modular, reusable functions
- Use IIFE pattern where appropriate for scoping

## Code Organization

### File Structure
```
kaf81.org/
├── index.html
├── apply.html              # Closed application page
├── apply-online.html       # Open application form page
├── apply-online.md         # Plan for opening applications
├── apply-offline.md        # Plan for closing applications
├── [other].html
├── css/
│   └── style.css
├── js/
│   ├── main.js
│   ├── contact.js
│   ├── form-validation.js
│   └── scholarship-application.js
└── images/
```

### Naming Conventions
- HTML files: lowercase with dashes (e.g., `apply-online.html`)
- CSS classes: lowercase with dashes (e.g., `.form-group`)
- JavaScript files: lowercase with dashes (e.g., `form-validation.js`)
- Images: lowercase with dashes (e.g., `scholarship-certificate.jpeg`)

## Response Constraints
- Do not remove any existing code unless necessary
- Do not remove comments or commented-out code unless necessary
- Do not change the formatting of existing code unless important for new functionality
- Do not change the formatting of imports unless necessary

## Accessibility Requirements (WCAG 2.1 AA)
- Use semantic HTML elements
- Include ARIA labels and roles where needed
- Ensure keyboard navigation works for all interactive elements
- Provide visible focus indicators
- Include skip navigation link
- Use proper form labels and error messages
- Maintain proper heading hierarchy
- Ensure color contrast meets AA standards
- Add `aria-current="page"` to current navigation items
- Use `aria-label` for icon-only buttons
- Include `role` attributes where semantic HTML isn't sufficient

## SEO Requirements
- Include unique meta descriptions on every page
- Use Open Graph tags for social sharing
- Use Twitter Card tags
- Include canonical URLs
- Add Schema.org structured data (JSON-LD):
  - Organization schema on homepage
  - FAQPage schema on FAQ page
  - BreadcrumbList schema on all pages
  - ContactPage schema on contact page
- All images must have descriptive alt text
- Use descriptive, keyword-rich page titles
- Include XML sitemap and robots.txt

## Performance Optimization (Lighthouse 100)
- Implement lazy loading for images (`loading="lazy"`)
- Optimize images (appropriate formats, sizes)
- Load Google Fonts with `display=swap` and preconnect
- Minimize and optimize CSS/JS (production)
- Use efficient CSS selectors
- Avoid render-blocking resources
- Defer non-critical JavaScript
- Use efficient data structures and algorithms
- Optimize network requests

## Form Handling
- Client-side validation before submission
- Accessible error messages (ARIA live regions)
- Loading states during submission
- Supabase integration for contact form and scholarship application form
- Use vanilla fetch() API (no axios or other libraries)
- Validate IIT-BHU email format (@itbhu.ac.in) for scholarship applications
- Validate checkbox groups (min/max selections) for funding sources and previous scholarships
- Handle multi-select fields by joining values with commas for database storage

## Best Practices
- Write concise, technical code
- Prefer functional and declarative programming patterns
- Avoid code duplication - use iteration and modularization
- Use descriptive variable names
- Structure files logically: HTML structure, then styling, then behavior
- Favor simplicity over cleverness
- Write code that is easy to understand and maintain

## Browser Support
- Support modern browsers (last 2 versions of Chrome, Firefox, Safari, Edge)
- Use feature detection where needed
- Provide fallbacks for unsupported features

## Testing Considerations
- Test keyboard navigation
- Test with screen readers
- Test on mobile devices
- Verify Lighthouse scores (aim for 100 in all categories)
- Test form validation and submission
- Verify all links work correctly
- Check color contrast ratios

## Documentation
- Update README.md when adding new features
- Comment complex code logic
- Document any external dependencies or setup requirements

## Git Practices
- Write clear, descriptive commit messages
- Don't commit temporary or build files
- Keep commits focused and atomic

## Application State Management
- Use two separate HTML files for application open/closed states:
  - `apply.html` - Display when applications are CLOSED (shows closed message)
  - `apply-online.html` - Display when applications are OPEN (shows application form)
- Do NOT use JavaScript toggles for open/closed state - manually update links when needed
- Use plan files (`apply-online.md` and `apply-offline.md`) as guides for switching states
- When switching states, update:
  - Homepage hero button link and text
  - Navigation links in all HTML files
  - Text links in content sections
- Keep both HTML files in repository - switch by updating links, not by hiding/showing elements

## Scholarship Application Form
- Form submits to Supabase `scholarship_applications` table
- Required fields: first_name, surname, roll_number, mobile_no, email_iit_bhu
- Email validation: email_iit_bhu must match @itbhu.ac.in domain pattern
- Conditional fields: JEE ranks for 1st year students, CPI for other years
- Multi-select fields: funding_sources and previous_kaf81_scholarship stored as comma-separated values
- Convert 0 values to null for optional numeric fields (JEE ranks, CPI, marks)
- Handle duplicate application errors gracefully with user-friendly messages

## Security and Public Repository
- Supabase anon keys are public by design (safe for client-side code)
- Sanitize documentation files (`.md`, `.sql`, `.txt`) before committing:
  - Replace real project IDs with placeholders (e.g., `YOUR_PROJECT_ID`)
  - Replace real API keys in docs with placeholders (e.g., `YOUR_ANON_KEY`)
- HTML files may contain actual Supabase credentials (anon keys) - this is acceptable as they're public by design
- Never commit service_role keys or other private credentials
- Use Row Level Security (RLS) policies in Supabase to protect data, not by hiding keys

## Google Analytics 4 (GA4) Integration

### GA4 Setup Requirements
- Include GA4 global site tag (gtag.js) in `<head>` section of ALL HTML pages
- Place GA4 script immediately after viewport meta tag
- Use async loading: `<script async src="https://www.googletagmanager.com/gtag/js?id=G-XXXXXXXXXX"></script>`
- Initialize dataLayer and gtag function before config
- Replace `G-XXXXXXXXXX` with actual GA4 Measurement ID
- GA4 script must load before any tracking modules

### GA4 Script Template
```html
<!-- Google tag (gtag.js) -->
<script async src="https://www.googletagmanager.com/gtag/js?id=G-XXXXXXXXXX"></script>
<script>
  window.dataLayer = window.dataLayer || [];
  function gtag(){dataLayer.push(arguments);}
  gtag('js', new Date());
  gtag('config', 'G-XXXXXXXXXX');
</script>
```

### Form Tracking Requirements
- Create `js/ga4-form-tracking.js` module for all form tracking
- Track the following events for ALL forms:
  1. `page_view` - Enhanced page view with form context
  2. `form_field_focus` - When user clicks into any form field
  3. `form_field_blur` - When user clicks out of any form field
  4. `form_submission_attempt` - Every time user clicks Submit button
  5. `form_submission_success` - When form successfully submits to backend
  6. `form_submission_failure` - When form submission fails (with error details)
  7. `form_submission_retry` - When user resubmits after a failure
- Integrate tracking into existing form submission handlers
- Load `ga4-form-tracking.js` before form handler scripts
- Use `window.GA4FormTracking.init(form)` to initialize tracking
- Call `window.GA4FormTracking.trackSuccess()` on successful submission
- Call `window.GA4FormTracking.trackFailure()` on submission errors

### Form Tracking Event Parameters
- `form_id`: Form element ID (e.g., "contact-form", "application-form")
- `form_name`: Human-readable form name (e.g., "Contact Form", "Application Form")
- `attempt_number`: Sequential attempt counter
- `time_on_form_seconds`: Time spent on form before submission
- `fields_filled`: Number of fields with values
- `total_fields`: Total number of form fields
- `completion_rate`: Percentage of fields filled
- `field_interactions_count`: Total field focus/blur events
- `unique_fields_interacted`: Number of unique fields touched
- For failures: `error_message`, `error_code`, `error_type`, `error_category`
- For success: Include sanitized form data (NO PII - no emails, names, addresses)

### Bot Detection and Tracking
- Create `js/ga4-bot-tracking.js` module for bot detection
- Detect and track AI bots, crawlers, and automated visitors
- Include bot tracking script in `<head>` after GA4 script on ALL pages
- Track `bot_visit` event with bot category and type
- Enhance `page_view` events with `visitor_type: "bot"` flag
- Bot categories to detect:
  - AI bots (ChatGPT, Claude, Perplexity, etc.)
  - Search engines (Googlebot, Bingbot, etc.)
  - Social media bots (Facebook, Twitter, LinkedIn, etc.)
  - SEO tools (Ahrefs, SEMrush, etc.)
  - Monitoring tools (UptimeRobot, Pingdom, etc.)
  - Generic bots (any automated tool)

### GA4 Event Tracking Standards
- Use descriptive, consistent event names (snake_case)
- Include relevant context in event parameters
- Never send PII (personally identifiable information) to GA4
- Truncate error messages to 100 characters max
- Sanitize form data before sending (remove emails, names, addresses)
- Include timestamps for time-sensitive events
- Use consistent parameter naming across all events
- Add `debug_mode` parameter when debug mode is enabled

### Offline Event Queuing
- Implement localStorage-based event queue for offline scenarios
- Queue events when gtag is unavailable or browser is offline
- Process queued events when connection is restored
- Limit queue size (recommended: 50 events max)
- Set event expiration (recommended: 7 days max age)
- Listen for online/offline events to trigger queue processing
- Periodically check connection and process queue (every 30 seconds)

### GA4 Configuration
- Use configuration object with debug mode flag
- Set `debugMode: true` during development, `false` in production
- Make Measurement ID configurable
- Allow runtime configuration updates via public API
- Include error handling for all GA4 operations
- Gracefully degrade if gtag is not available

### Privacy and Data Handling
- NEVER send PII to GA4 (no emails, names, phone numbers, addresses)
- Sanitize all form data before tracking
- Truncate user agent strings to 100 characters
- Use aggregated data only (counts, percentages, categories)
- Comply with GDPR and privacy regulations
- Document data collection in privacy policy
- Allow users to opt-out if required by law

### GA4 File Structure
```
js/
├── ga4-form-tracking.js    # Form interaction and submission tracking
├── ga4-bot-tracking.js     # Bot detection and tracking
└── [form-handler].js       # Form submission handlers (integrated with GA4)
```

### GA4 Integration Checklist
When adding GA4 to a new static website:
- [ ] Add GA4 global site tag to all HTML pages in `<head>`
- [ ] Create `js/ga4-form-tracking.js` module
- [ ] Create `js/ga4-bot-tracking.js` module
- [ ] Integrate form tracking into all form handlers
- [ ] Test all tracking events in browser console
- [ ] Verify events appear in GA4 Real-Time reports
- [ ] Test offline event queuing
- [ ] Verify no PII is sent to GA4
- [ ] Set `debugMode: false` for production
- [ ] Document GA4 Measurement ID location

### GA4 Testing Requirements
- Test all event tracking in browser console
- Verify events appear in GA4 Real-Time reports
- Test form submission success/failure tracking
- Test bot detection with different user agents
- Test offline event queuing
- Verify error handling doesn't break form functionality
- Test with debug mode enabled/disabled
- Verify no JavaScript errors in console

## Specific Rules for This Project
- Do not add any CSS frameworks (Bootstrap, Tailwind, etc.)
- Do not add any JavaScript frameworks or libraries (React, Vue, jQuery, etc.)
- Do not use CSS preprocessors (SASS, LESS, etc.)
- Keep all code in the repository - no build steps required
- Use only HTML, CSS, and vanilla JavaScript
- Google Fonts are allowed (external resource)
- Supabase is allowed (backend service for contact form and scholarship applications)
- Google Analytics 4 (gtag.js) is allowed (analytics tracking)

